maia-kmod: close remaining mmap_done TOCTOU race with a mutex

0003 serialized vm_close/cacheinv against mmap_done/vm_start with a
spinlock, but had to release that spinlock around remap_pfn_range()
in maia_sdr_rxbuffer_mmap() (remap_pfn_range() can sleep -- allocating
page-table pages with GFP_KERNEL -- so it cannot be called while
holding a spinlock). That left the original race open at its
narrowest but most consequential point: two concurrent mmap() calls
on the rxbuffer device (e.g. from two simultaneous iiod client
contexts, one over the USB FunctionFS backend and one over the
network/RNDIS backend) can both observe mmap_done == 0, both proceed
to remap_pfn_range() the same physical DMA buffer into two different
processes' address spaces, and then both write mmap_done/vm_start --
whichever finishes last silently wins, leaving vm_start pointing at
the wrong process's virtual address for the other mapping.

This matters because vm_start feeds directly into v7_dma_inv_range()
from IOCTL_CACHEINV: a cache-line invalidate (discard, not writeback)
against a stale/wrong virtual address silently drops live data
instead of writing anything recognizable, which is a plausible source
of the "wild write into general RAM" corruption signature under
concurrent RF/USB load documented in doc/crashnano.md (repeating
fixed value at varying physical addresses -- consistent with
cache-line-granularity corruption, not a raw pointer write).

Fix: replace the spinlock with a mutex and hold it across the entire
check-then-remap-then-set sequence in maia_sdr_rxbuffer_mmap(), not
just around the individual reads/writes. A mutex is safe to hold
across remap_pfn_range() since both run in sleepable process context
(mmap() and munmap() are never called from atomic/interrupt context),
which is exactly what let 0003's spinlock-based attempt leave this
window open in the first place.

Lock-ordering check: .mmap and vm_ops->close both run with mm->mmap_lock
held by the core mm code, so both acquisitions here nest inside
mmap_lock (mmap_lock -> drvdata->lock). The only other acquirer is
IOCTL_CACHEINV, which holds no mm lock on entry, so the ordering is
one-directional as long as nothing under drvdata->lock reaches back
into mm code. v7_dma_inv_range() (arch/arm/mm/cache-v7.S) is a
straight-line loop of "mcr p15" cache-maintenance-by-MVA instructions
with no page-table walk or C code involved; arm_cache_outer_inv_range()
(arch/arm/mm/outercache.c) dispatches to the PL310 L2 controller driver
over MMIO on a physical address. Neither touches mm_struct or
mmap_lock, so no ABBA deadlock is possible. arm_cache_outer_inv_range()
is also moved outside drvdata->lock in this patch: it only depends on
mem_base_addr, which is fixed at probe time, not on vm_start/mmap_done,
so it was never protected by the lock in substance, only in position --
moving it out shortens the critical section without changing behavior.

Dropping IRQ-off: 0003 used spin_lock_irqsave, but nothing in this
driver touches mmap_done/vm_start from interrupt or other atomic
context -- the only writers/readers are .mmap, vm_ops->close, and the
ioctl handler, all process context. The irqsave was therefore
defensive rather than load-bearing, and a plain mutex is sufficient
now that the critical section needs to span a sleeping call anyway.

Status: written and cross-compiled clean against the pinned kernel
(6.12.77, output/pciesdr7010/build/linux-custom), not yet flashed or
tested on hardware. This is the third attempt at this specific race
(after 0002 and 0003, both already applied and both insufficient --
the crash reproduced on pciesdr7010 with both in place); treat it as
unproven against the actual crash until tested against the boot/
login-prompt reproduction recipe in doc/crashnano.md.

